%I A350555 #31 Sep 25 2026 08:47:48
%S A350555 365,29,79,679,3159,83,473,638,434,89,17,79,31,41,517,111,305,23,73,
%T A350555 61,37,19,89,41,833,53,86,13,23,67,71,83,475,59,41,1,1,1,1,89
%N A350555 Numerators of Conway's PIGAME.
%C A350555 These rational numbers, a(n)/A350556(n), define the FRACTRAN program PIGAME that generates the decimal expansion of Pi (A000796).
%C A350555 Conway proves that, when this program is started at 89*2^k (with k >= 0), the next power of 2 to appear is 2^Pi(k), where Pi(k) = floor(Pi*10^k) mod 10 is the k-th digit after the decimal point in the decimal expansion of Pi. [Corrected 2^k to 89*2^k: The initial value 2^k as written in Conway's original paper doesn't work for k > 9 due to A350556(38) = 1024; it must be multiplied by 89. For k < 10, the first iteration adds the missing factor 89. See also Kaushik, Murphy, and Weed. - _M. F. Hasler_, Sep 15 2026]
%H A350555 J. H. Conway, "FRACTRAN: A Simple Universal Programming Language for Arithmetic", in J. C. Lagarias, ed., <a href="https://bookstore.ams.org/mbk-78">The Ultimate Challenge: The 3x+1 Problem</a>, American Mathematical Society, 2010, p. 249, and in T. M. Cover and B. Gopinath, eds, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-4808-8_2">Open Problems in Communication and Computation</a>, Springer, New York, NY, 1987, pp. 4-26.
%H A350555 Khushi Kaushik, Tommy Murphy, and David Weed, <a href="https://doi.org/10.33232/BIMS.0095.23.34">Computing sqrt(2) with FRACTRAN</a>, Irish Math. Soc. Bull., 95 (2025), 23-34 (warning: theorem 3.1 is missing the last fraction 1/97); arXiv:<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.16185">2412.16185</a> [cs.PL], 2024.
%H A350555 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FRACTRAN">FRACTRAN</a>.
%e A350555 From _M. F. Hasler_, Sep 24 2026: (Start)
%e A350555 The fractions a(n)/A350556(n) are (365/46, 29/161, 79/575, 679/451, 3159/413, 83/407, 473/371, 638/355, 434/335, 89/235, 17/209, 79/122, 31/183, 41/115, 517/89, 111/83, 305/79, 23/73, 73/71, 61/67, 37/61, 19/59, 89/57, 41/53, 833/47, 53/43, 86/41, 13/38, 23/37, 67/31, 71/29, 83/19, 475/17, 59/13, 41/291, 1/7, 1/11, 1/1024, 1/97, 89/1).
%e A350555 See A395539 for examples how the PIGAME program/successor function is applied to initial values. (End)
%Y A350555 Cf. A000796, A202138, A350556 (denominators), A395539 (the PIGAME successor function).
